Today, large AI training jobs crash for a broad range of reasons and recovery often involves significant amounts of human intervention. At Meta, we observed that 5-20% of our job failures are actually due to kernel bugs in NIC drivers. Unlike other class of bugs, these are challenging to diagnose because we lack visibility into this aspect of the kernel. In this paper, we describe RDMATracer, our eBPF-based framework for tracking RDMA-specific systems to necessarily capture information to diagnose these bugs. Our RDMATracer has been run in production for several years now. We had to explore a set of design choices to scale collection and develop an observability first pipeline for enriching kernel with additional user space context to enable our DevOps team to fix and rectify these problems. We identify, from our experience developing and using RDMATracer, a set of promising directions for future research.